Creating controllers in Spring MVC involves understanding how to handle different types of HTTP requests and how to extract data from those requests. Below, I'll provide a comprehensive guide on creating various types of controllers, handling different HTTP methods, and using different ways to extract input from requests.

1. Basic Controller Setup
Explanation:
A Spring MVC controller is a class annotated with @Controller or @RestController. The @RestController is a convenience annotation that combines @Controller and @ResponseBody, meaning that the methods in the class will return data directly as a response body.

2. Handling Different HTTP Methods
GET Request with Query Parameters
Explanation:
Use @RequestParam to extract query parameters from the URL.

Code Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {

    // Example URL: /api/products?category=electronics&sort=asc
    @GetMapping
    public List<Product> getProducts(@RequestParam String category, @RequestParam String sort) {
        // Logic to fetch products based on category and sort order
        return productService.getProductsByCategoryAndSort(category, sort);
    }
}
GET Request with Path Variables
Explanation:
Use @PathVariable to extract values from the URI path.

Code Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {

    // Example URL: /api/products/123
    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public Product getProductById(@PathVariable Long id) {
        // Logic to fetch a product by its ID
        return productService.getProductById(id);
    }
}
POST Request with Request Body
Explanation:
Use @RequestBody to bind the HTTP request body to a domain object.

Code Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {

    // Example URL: /api/products
    // Example Request Body: {"name": "Laptop", "price": 999.99}
    @PostMapping
    public Product createProduct(@RequestBody Product product) {
        // Logic to save the product
        return productService.saveProduct(product);
    }
}
PUT Request with Path Variable and Request Body
Explanation:
Use @PathVariable for the resource identifier and @RequestBody for the updated data.

Code Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {

    // Example URL: /api/products/123
    // Example Request Body: {"name": "Updated Laptop", "price": 1099.99}
    @PutMapping("/{id}")
    public Product updateProduct(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody Product product) {
        // Logic to update the product
        return productService.updateProduct(id, product);
    }
}
DELETE Request with Path Variable
Explanation:
Use @PathVariable to specify which resource to delete.

Code Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {

    // Example URL: /api/products/123
    @DeleteMapping("/{id}")
    public void deleteProduct(@PathVariable Long id) {
        // Logic to delete the product
        productService.deleteProduct(id);
    }
}
PATCH Request with Path Variable and Request Body
Explanation:
Use @PathVariable for the resource identifier and @RequestBody for partial updates.

Code Example:

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/products")
public class ProductController {

    // Example URL: /api/products/123
    // Example Request Body: {"price": 899.99}
    @PatchMapping("/{id}")
    public Product partiallyUpdateProduct(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestBody Map<String, Object> updates) {
        // Logic to partially update the product
        return productService.partiallyUpdateProduct(id, updates);
    }
}
3. Using @ModelAttribute
Explanation:
@ModelAttribute is used to bind request parameters to a model object, often used in web applications with forms.

Code Example:

@Controller
@RequestMapping("/products")
public class ProductWebController {

    // Example URL: /products/form
    @GetMapping("/form")
    public String showForm(Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("product", new Product());
        return "productForm"; // Returns a view name
    }

    // Example URL: /products
    // Example Form Submission: name=Laptop&price=999.99
    @PostMapping
    public String submitForm(@ModelAttribute Product product) {
        // Logic to save the product
        productService.saveProduct(product);
        return "redirect:/products";
    }
}
Thymeleaf Form Example:

<!-- productForm.html -->
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html xmlns:th="http://www.thymeleaf.org">
<head>
    <title>Product Form</title>
</head>
<body>
    <form th:action="@{/products}" th:object="${product}" method="post">
        <label for="name">Name:</label>
        <input type="text" id="name" th:field="*{name}" />
        <br/>
        <label for="price">Price:</label>
        <input type="text" id="price" th:field="*{price}" />
        <br/>
        <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
</body>
</html>
4. Summary
@RequestParam: Used for query parameters.
@PathVariable: Used for path parameters.
@RequestBody: Used for request body data.
@ModelAttribute: Used for form data binding in web applications.
Each of these annotations serves a specific purpose in handling HTTP requests and extracting data, allowing you to build flexible and powerful web applications with Spring MVC. For further reading, consider exploring the official Spring MVC documentation.
